    "abstract":  "\u003cjats:title\u003eAbstract\u003c/jats:title\u003e\n               \u003cjats:p\u003eThis chapter explores the story of Dandin in the Bay of Bengal and Burma through unchartered literary sources. It also problematizes the ambivalent categorization of Dandin’s Sanskrit classic in a cultural landscape dominated by Pali Buddhism. The elusive presence of Dandin’s Mirror in the region is traced in the records of monastic manuscript collections and elite monastic curricula, where it occupies a prominent position. Pali adaptations of the Mirror, mainly Sangharakkhita’s Lucid Poetics and its commentaries, are also examined in connection to local Buddhist scholarship in Pali and Burmese, showing the influence of Dandin’s system of ornaments beyond the realm of poetry. The final section explores the destiny of Dandin’s Mirror in tandem with Dharmadasa’s work on riddles, the Adornment of the Connoisseur’s Mouth (Vidagdhamukhamaṇḍana). This chapter shows that, by adapting certain features of the Mirror’s discourse on poetics to philosophy, pedagogy, and ritual, the Mirror achieved a paracanonical status among Buddhist literati in the Bay of Bengal.\u003c/jats:p\u003e",
